is going to take up the remainder of the podcast.

143
00:08:02,540 --> 00:08:04,380
So let's get started, shall we?

144
00:08:04,380 --> 00:08:27,900
One of the things that often comes up

145
00:08:27,900 --> 00:08:29,540
when we're talking about parents and scouting

146
00:08:29,540 --> 00:08:31,860
is their role in the advancement process.

147
00:08:31,860 --> 00:08:34,700
A lot of times parents- especially if younger scouts-

148
00:08:34,700 --> 00:08:37,260
will be concerned when other scouts seem

149
00:08:37,260 --> 00:08:39,940
to bypass their son in advancement,

150
00:08:39,940 --> 00:08:41,860
And they ask a lot of questions about this,

151
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:43,740
because if they were involved in cub scouts,

152
00:08:43,740 --> 00:08:48,220
advancement is done more or less by the entire den,

153
00:08:48,220 --> 00:08:49,740
And so they've had several years of that

154
00:08:49,740 --> 00:08:51,140
and they come to scouting and they find out:

155
00:08:51,140 --> 00:08:53,060
oh, that's not exactly the way that it happens

156
00:08:53,060 --> 00:08:54,100
in a scout troop,

157
00:08:54,100 --> 00:08:55,780
And that can be a little confusing.

158
00:08:55,780 --> 00:08:59,220
And even if they do understand that the scout troop

159
00:08:59,220 --> 00:09:01,700
is a little different, moms and dads

160
00:09:01,740 --> 00:09:03,420
can get a little overheated when it comes

161
00:09:03,420 --> 00:09:04,620
to their son's advancement.

162
00:09:04,620 --> 00:09:07,020
Sometimes they can be gently dissuaded from this

163
00:09:07,020 --> 00:09:10,380
by a quiet reminder of who does what and why,

164
00:09:10,380 --> 00:09:12,060
and sometimes not.

165
00:09:12,060 --> 00:09:15,740
So here's my golden rule for working with parents

166
00:09:15,740 --> 00:09:16,580
and scouting.

167
00:09:16,580 --> 00:09:19,780
An important thing to know is that a scout

168
00:09:19,780 --> 00:09:22,540
should never be made to suffer for anything

169
00:09:22,540 --> 00:09:23,700
beyond their control,

170
00:09:23,700 --> 00:09:27,220
And the attitudes and actions of their parents

171
00:09:27,220 --> 00:09:31,580
are usually going to be beyond the scout's control, correct?

172
00:09:31,580 --> 00:09:33,300
You don't choose your parents

173
00:09:33,300 --> 00:09:36,220
And you don't get to tell your parents how to act.

174
00:09:36,220 --> 00:09:37,620
So that bear is repeating.

175
00:09:37,620 --> 00:09:40,660
A scout should never be made to suffer

176
00:09:40,660 --> 00:09:42,980
for things beyond their control,

177
00:09:42,980 --> 00:09:45,700
And the attitudes and actions of their parents

178
00:09:45,700 --> 00:09:48,140
are definitely beyond their control.

179
00:09:48,140 --> 00:09:50,900
We don't want scouts to feel put out

180
00:09:50,900 --> 00:09:52,780
or to suffer for the attitudes and actions

181
00:09:52,780 --> 00:09:53,700
of their parents,

182
00:09:53,700 --> 00:09:55,420
And that's why I'm going to encourage you:

183
00:09:55,420 --> 00:09:56,700
when it comes to advancement,

184
00:09:56,700 --> 00:09:59,780
don't put on the brakes and don't press the gas pedal.

185
00:09:59,780 --> 00:10:02,339
The scouts themselves will figure out

186
00:10:02,339 --> 00:10:04,459
how much they're interested in advancing,

187
00:10:04,459 --> 00:10:05,900
how fast they want to go.

188
00:10:05,900 --> 00:10:08,020
Parents don't always understand that

189
00:10:08,020 --> 00:10:10,220
and parents may get a little overheated.

190
00:10:10,220 --> 00:10:11,579
But, like I said,

191
00:10:11,579 --> 00:10:13,740
resist the urge to put on the brake,

192
00:10:13,740 --> 00:10:15,660
resist the urge to push the gas pedal.

193
00:10:15,660 --> 00:10:18,060
let scouts individually figure out

194
00:10:18,060 --> 00:10:20,260
how fast they want to advance

195
00:10:20,260 --> 00:10:23,540
and accept the fact that in some cases

196
00:10:23,540 --> 00:10:25,180
parents are going to be pushing them,

197
00:10:25,180 --> 00:10:27,660
and don't make your scouts suffer

198
00:10:27,660 --> 00:10:29,660
because their parents are being pushy.

199
00:10:29,660 --> 00:10:31,780
When we're talking to parents about advancement,

200
00:10:31,780 --> 00:10:33,380
here are a couple of key thoughts for them.

201
00:10:33,380 --> 00:10:36,060
Scouts can't fall behind or be bypassed

202
00:10:36,060 --> 00:10:37,500
when it comes to advancement.

203
00:10:37,500 --> 00:10:39,500
Scouts do things when they want,

204
00:10:39,500 --> 00:10:40,980
when they're ready to do them.

205
00:10:40,980 --> 00:10:42,819
Some do them faster than others,

206
00:10:42,819 --> 00:10:44,380
some do them slower,

207
00:10:44,380 --> 00:10:46,939
Sometimes they don't do them at all,

208
00:10:46,939 --> 00:10:49,819
And any of one of those three ways

209
00:10:49,819 --> 00:10:52,939
of being a scout is perfectly acceptable to me.

210
00:10:52,939 --> 00:10:55,260
During the years that they're in a scout troop,

211
00:10:55,260 --> 00:10:56,540
some of their fellow scouts

212
00:10:56,540 --> 00:10:58,280
are going to advance faster,

213
00:10:58,280 --> 00:11:00,480
but that is not necessarily a reflection

214
00:11:00,480 --> 00:11:03,680
on an individual scout's intelligence,

215
00:11:03,680 --> 00:11:05,360
interest or engagement.

216
00:11:05,360 --> 00:11:08,000
So that's one thing I like to share with parents.

217
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,720
that points out that when we're talking

218
00:11:10,720 --> 00:11:12,439
about scouts in a scout troop,

219
00:11:12,439 --> 00:11:14,520
they can advance at any speed they wish

220
00:11:14,520 --> 00:11:17,800
and they can choose to advance not at all.

221
00:11:19,579 --> 00:11:23,079
Now, usually scouts think that's a great idea,

222
00:11:23,079 --> 00:11:25,600
until a scout starts advancing too fast

223
00:11:25,600 --> 00:11:26,959
and they want to put the brakes on.

224
00:11:26,960 --> 00:11:28,760
So there's only one way to earn a rank,

225
00:11:28,760 --> 00:11:30,440
There's only one way to earn a merit badge

226
00:11:30,440 --> 00:11:33,400
or any other advancement in scouting,

227
00:11:33,400 --> 00:11:36,440
and that is to complete the requirements for them.

228
00:11:36,440 --> 00:11:38,520
If we agree that there is no such thing

229
00:11:38,520 --> 00:11:41,360
as completing the requirements too slowly,

230
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:44,440
we also must agree that there is no such thing

231
00:11:44,440 --> 00:11:47,160
as completing the requirements too quickly.

232
00:11:47,160 --> 00:11:49,880
So have plenty of room in your mind

233
00:11:49,880 --> 00:11:52,000
and in your heart and in your program

234
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:53,920
for scouts who want to advance quickly

235
00:11:53,920 --> 00:11:55,840
and scouts who want to advance slowly,

236
00:11:55,840 --> 00:11:58,200
And those will not always be the same scouts,

237
00:11:58,200 --> 00:12:01,520
You know. one year slow, next year fast,

238
00:12:01,520 --> 00:12:04,840
next year slow. it's the scout's decision.

239
00:12:04,840 --> 00:12:06,600
The other really important thing to understand-

240
00:12:06,600 --> 00:12:07,920
and a lot of parents don't-

241
00:12:07,920 --> 00:12:10,260
is that the fulfillment of merit badge requirements

242
00:12:10,260 --> 00:12:12,800
is a matter for merit badge counselors

243
00:12:12,800 --> 00:12:14,480
and scouts to decide

244
00:12:14,480 --> 00:12:17,560
and not scoutmasters or assistant scoutmasters,

245
00:12:17,560 --> 00:12:20,200
except if they also are in the role

246
00:12:20,200 --> 00:12:21,760
of a merit badge counselor.

247
00:12:21,760 --> 00:12:24,080
What you think about a given requirement

248
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:26,080
in a given situation for a given merit badge

249
00:12:26,080 --> 00:12:29,200
with a given scout is really doesn't amount to anything,

250
00:12:29,200 --> 00:12:32,300
because the counselor is going to make that decision.

251
00:12:32,300 --> 00:12:34,680
And in addition to those two thoughts

252
00:12:34,680 --> 00:12:38,720
I would also get out the Guide to Advancement 2015

253
00:12:38,720 --> 00:12:42,200
and make sure parents have all read

254
00:12:42,200 --> 00:12:45,080
a reasonably brief but a very important statement

255
00:12:45,080 --> 00:12:48,440
in the Guide to Advancement called personal growth

256
00:12:48,440 --> 00:12:51,840
is the prime consideration for advancement.

257
00:12:51,840 --> 00:12:54,440
And that is in the Guide to Advancement.

258
00:12:54,440 --> 00:12:55,920
it's all numbers right,

259
00:12:55,920 --> 00:12:57,680
So get your pencils ready.

260
00:12:57,680 --> 00:13:02,680
It's 2.0.0.3,. personal growth is prime consideration.

261
00:13:03,400 --> 00:13:05,620
It's brief, but it's very to the point

262
00:13:05,620 --> 00:13:07,760
and it's important thing for parents to know.

263
00:13:07,760 --> 00:13:10,520
And it's brief enough for me to read it in its entirety.

264
00:13:10,520 --> 00:13:13,000
Quote: scouting skills-

265
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:16,080
what a young person learns to do- are important,

266
00:13:16,080 --> 00:13:19,000
but not as important as the growth achieved

267
00:13:19,040 --> 00:13:21,920
through participating in a unit program.

268
00:13:21,920 --> 00:13:26,160
The concern is for total, well-rounded development.

269
00:13:26,160 --> 00:13:28,640
Age-appropriate, surmountable hurdles

270
00:13:28,640 --> 00:13:32,240
are placed before members and as they face them,

271
00:13:32,240 --> 00:13:35,320
they learn about themselves and gain confidence.

272
00:13:35,320 --> 00:13:38,140
Success is achieved when we fulfill

273
00:13:38,140 --> 00:13:40,480
the BSA mission statement

274
00:13:40,480 --> 00:13:43,560
and when we accomplish the aims of scouting,

275
00:13:43,560 --> 00:13:46,240
Those being character development,

276
00:13:46,280 --> 00:13:50,080
citizenship training and mental and physical fitness.

277
00:13:50,080 --> 00:13:52,040
We know we are on the right track

278
00:13:52,040 --> 00:13:55,200
when we see youth accepting responsibility,

279
00:13:55,200 --> 00:13:56,980
demonstrating self-reliance

280
00:13:56,980 --> 00:13:59,080
and caring for themselves and others,

281
00:13:59,080 --> 00:14:03,320
When they learn to weave scouting ideals into their lives

282
00:14:03,320 --> 00:14:06,560
and when we can see they will be positive contributors

283
00:14:06,560 --> 00:14:08,520
to our American society.

284
00:14:08,520 --> 00:14:10,480
Though certainly goal-oriented

285
00:14:10,480 --> 00:14:12,640
advancement is not a competition.

286
00:14:12,640 --> 00:14:16,040
Rather it is a joint effort involving the leaders,

287
00:14:16,040 --> 00:14:18,360
the members, other volunteers-

288
00:14:18,360 --> 00:14:20,079
such as merit badge counselors

289
00:14:20,079 --> 00:14:22,640
or venturing consultants- and the family.

290
00:14:22,640 --> 00:14:25,640
Though much is done individually at their own pace,

291
00:14:25,640 --> 00:14:27,780
youth often work together in groups

292
00:14:27,780 --> 00:14:30,199
to focus on achievements and electives

293
00:14:30,199 --> 00:14:32,719
at Cub Scout, then meetings, for example,

294
00:14:32,719 --> 00:14:36,680
or participate in a Boy Scout camp out or Sea Scout cruise.

295
00:14:36,680 --> 00:14:39,079
As they do this, we must recognize

296
00:14:39,079 --> 00:14:42,120
each young person's unique combination

297
00:14:42,120 --> 00:14:44,420
of strengths and weaknesses.

298
00:14:44,460 --> 00:14:47,780
As watchful leaders, either adult or youth,

299
00:14:47,780 --> 00:14:50,300
we lend assistance as called for

300
00:14:50,300 --> 00:14:52,580
and encourage members to help each other

301
00:14:52,580 --> 00:14:55,580
according to their abilities. unquote.

302
00:14:55,580 --> 00:14:59,140
So parents should see and read and understand

303
00:14:59,140 --> 00:15:01,420
that section of the guide to advancement.

304
00:15:01,420 --> 00:15:03,460
They wanna help and that's good.

305
00:15:03,460 --> 00:15:06,100
but they do get anxious and that's difficult,

306
00:15:06,100 --> 00:15:08,400
but that's also very common.

307
00:15:08,400 --> 00:15:09,980
Most scouts are going to go through

308
00:15:09,980 --> 00:15:11,940
a period of inactivity. advancement-wise

309
00:15:11,940 --> 00:15:13,140
It's no big deal,

310
00:15:13,180 --> 00:15:15,260
but parents don't always know that.

311
00:15:15,260 --> 00:15:17,819
It's difficult to redirect their efforts sometimes.

312
00:15:17,819 --> 00:15:20,100
but most scouts are going to survive

313
00:15:20,100 --> 00:15:22,819
and thrive in spite of their parents.

314
00:15:22,819 --> 00:15:24,260
And I've had a lot of scouts

315
00:15:24,260 --> 00:15:26,620
who just aren't interested in advancement at all,

316
00:15:26,620 --> 00:15:28,900
Sometimes for a year, sometimes more,

317
00:15:28,900 --> 00:15:30,500
and they go on to become Eagle Scouts

318
00:15:30,500 --> 00:15:32,620
because when they get it in their minds

319
00:15:32,620 --> 00:15:34,620
that it's something they wanna do.

320
00:15:34,620 --> 00:15:36,020
you really can't stop them,

321
00:15:36,020 --> 00:15:38,100
And I've worked with many, many parents

322
00:15:38,100 --> 00:15:40,740
who I would consider to be overly involved

323
00:15:40,740 --> 00:15:42,620
in their son's scouting experience

324
00:15:42,620 --> 00:15:45,740
And they usually back off and they calm down a little bit

325
00:15:45,740 --> 00:15:48,980
if I can talk to them about the nature of scout advancement.

326
00:15:48,980 --> 00:15:52,660
but sometimes I don't get anywhere, and that's fine

327
00:15:52,660 --> 00:15:54,800
because we're charged to work with our scouts

328
00:15:54,800 --> 00:15:55,820
as they come to us.

329
00:15:55,820 --> 00:15:58,180
We can't change what happens at home.

330
00:15:58,180 --> 00:16:00,140
Remember my little golden rule:

331
00:16:00,140 --> 00:16:02,340
scouts don't get to choose their parents,

332
00:16:02,340 --> 00:16:05,220
so we don't make them suffer for what their parents do.

333
00:16:05,220 --> 00:16:07,260
So let's change our tune here for a moment,

334
00:16:07,260 --> 00:16:10,060
because before I conclude this three-part series

335
00:16:10,060 --> 00:16:12,180
on parents and scouting,

336
00:16:12,180 --> 00:16:15,260
I wanted to make some comments about what it's like

337
00:16:15,260 --> 00:16:18,260
to be a parent and a scouter at the same time,

338
00:16:18,260 --> 00:16:20,700
because characteristics that make a good parent

339
00:16:20,700 --> 00:16:23,860
are very similar to those that make a good scouter.

340
00:16:23,860 --> 00:16:26,700
But we're not going to impose the same expectations

341
00:16:26,700 --> 00:16:30,980
or authority we have for our own children on our scouts.

342
00:16:30,980 --> 00:16:33,580
As adult volunteers in scouting

343
00:16:33,580 --> 00:16:36,860
we have to be sure that we divide our roles

344
00:16:36,860 --> 00:16:39,180
as parent and a scouter

345
00:16:39,180 --> 00:16:42,820
and that we make sure that we understand those two roles

346
00:16:42,820 --> 00:16:45,300
and we do our best not to let them mix together.

347
00:16:45,300 --> 00:16:48,140
Parents are likely to be more demanding

348
00:16:48,140 --> 00:16:50,820
and more critical and more emotional

349
00:16:50,820 --> 00:16:52,900
when they deal with their own children.

350
00:16:52,900 --> 00:16:55,860
Separating the expectations that you have for your child

351
00:16:55,860 --> 00:16:58,540
as your child being their parent

352
00:16:58,540 --> 00:17:00,980
and separating the expectations that you have

353
00:17:00,980 --> 00:17:04,740
for your child as a scout is a little tricky.

354
00:17:04,740 --> 00:17:07,260
You're going to do and say things as a scouter

355
00:17:07,260 --> 00:17:09,300
that will make your child proud

356
00:17:09,300 --> 00:17:11,579
and you'll probably say and do some things

357
00:17:11,579 --> 00:17:13,180
that make them cringe

358
00:17:13,180 --> 00:17:16,660
and they will no doubt return the favor for you.

359
00:17:16,660 --> 00:17:18,140
And somewhere along the way,

360
00:17:18,140 --> 00:17:19,940
occupying both of those roles

361
00:17:19,940 --> 00:17:22,020
is going to cause some tension.

362
00:17:22,020 --> 00:17:25,020
Let's remember your child's reasons for being a scout

363
00:17:25,020 --> 00:17:28,960
are different from your reasons for being a scouter.

364
00:17:28,960 --> 00:17:31,260
Now, sure, you want to spend time with your child

365
00:17:31,260 --> 00:17:34,100
but, as we'd expect, they're probably more interested

366
00:17:34,100 --> 00:17:35,500
in spending time with their friends

367
00:17:35,500 --> 00:17:36,680
when they're with scouts.

368
00:17:36,680 --> 00:17:39,880
We also need to understand that parents and children

369
00:17:39,880 --> 00:17:43,000
have inside information about each other.

370
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,920
They know what goes on at home and at school and at work.

371
00:17:45,920 --> 00:17:49,360
They know each other's history and strengths and weaknesses,

372
00:17:49,360 --> 00:17:51,960
and inside information is going to skew your judgment

373
00:17:51,960 --> 00:17:54,800
as a scouter, because you know things about your child

374
00:17:54,800 --> 00:17:56,200
that nobody else knows,

375
00:17:56,200 --> 00:17:59,120
and it's going to skew your child's objectivity

376
00:17:59,120 --> 00:18:01,600
when it comes to your role as a scouter

377
00:18:01,600 --> 00:18:03,640
because they know things about you

378
00:18:03,640 --> 00:18:05,080
that nobody else knows.

379
00:18:05,080 --> 00:18:07,159
And, if you haven't figured it out already,

380
00:18:07,159 --> 00:18:09,600
every parent has a blind spot,

381
00:18:09,600 --> 00:18:12,399
and sometimes several, when it comes to their own children.

382
00:18:12,399 --> 00:18:14,360
So having a trusted fellow scout

383
00:18:14,360 --> 00:18:16,360
or help you watch for blind spots

384
00:18:16,360 --> 00:18:20,159
and lend some objectivity to this complex pair of relationships

385
00:18:20,159 --> 00:18:21,800
that you have with your child

386
00:18:21,800 --> 00:18:25,179
can make things a lot easier for you and for your child.

387
00:18:25,179 --> 00:18:27,960
Of course, you will return the favor

388
00:18:27,960 --> 00:18:30,560
and watch for their blind spots with their children,

389
00:18:30,560 --> 00:18:32,199
who are involved with scouts too.

390
00:18:32,199 --> 00:18:34,480
Now if you are a parent and a scouter

391
00:18:34,480 --> 00:18:36,720
and you have a child in your unit,

392
00:18:36,720 --> 00:18:38,160
I'd urge you not to switch

393
00:18:38,160 --> 00:18:41,160
between the roles of scouter and parent all that often.

394
00:18:41,160 --> 00:18:44,040
Don't treat a scout as your child one moment

395
00:18:44,040 --> 00:18:46,280
and a scout the next.

396
00:18:46,280 --> 00:18:47,600
Have a consistent approach

397
00:18:47,600 --> 00:18:50,240
that you and your child agree to ahead of time.

398
00:18:50,240 --> 00:18:52,960
Some things I see when young scouts and new scouters

399
00:18:52,960 --> 00:18:54,640
go on their first couple of camping trips

400
00:18:54,640 --> 00:18:57,200
are good illustration of opportunities

401
00:18:57,200 --> 00:18:59,440
to keep the two relationships separate.

402
00:18:59,440 --> 00:19:01,800
So let's say, your child wanders over

403
00:19:01,800 --> 00:19:03,960
and asks for something during a camping trip.

404
00:19:03,960 --> 00:19:05,640
Maybe they forgot to pack something.

405
00:19:05,640 --> 00:19:09,160
The temptation is, of course, to switch into the parent role

406
00:19:09,160 --> 00:19:12,080
and run off and make sure they get whatever they forgot,

407
00:19:12,080 --> 00:19:15,600
But if a scout approached a scouter with the same request,

408
00:19:15,600 --> 00:19:17,040
would we do that?

409
00:19:17,040 --> 00:19:18,800
We'd probably end up telling them

410
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:21,680
that scouts are expected to bring their own gear

411
00:19:21,680 --> 00:19:25,200
and ask them why they would expect us to have one for them.

412
00:19:25,200 --> 00:19:26,960
And this happens too with food.

413
00:19:26,960 --> 00:19:28,320
The child doesn't like the food

414
00:19:28,320 --> 00:19:31,100
His patrol is preparing. he comes and he finds his parent

415
00:19:31,100 --> 00:19:33,220
and tells them, and usually the parent

416
00:19:33,220 --> 00:19:34,460
shares some food with them.

417
00:19:34,460 --> 00:19:36,580
So the idea is that if you're a parent

418
00:19:36,580 --> 00:19:37,420
and that happens to you,

419
00:19:37,420 --> 00:19:40,180
you have to ask yourself: what would a scouter do?

420
00:19:40,180 --> 00:19:42,460
What would you tell somebody who wasn't your child?

421
00:19:42,460 --> 00:19:43,300
Oh no,

422
00:19:43,300 --> 00:19:44,940
This is what you brought for food.

423
00:19:44,940 --> 00:19:46,620
so that's what you're gonna have.

424
00:19:46,620 --> 00:19:48,640
And, like I said, those problems

425
00:19:48,640 --> 00:19:50,020
are probably gonna come up.

426
00:19:50,020 --> 00:19:52,620
but it would be a good idea. if you're really smart,

427
00:19:52,620 --> 00:19:54,620
you discuss them with your child ahead of time

428
00:19:54,620 --> 00:19:57,060
and you explain that they need to accept

429
00:19:57,060 --> 00:19:59,100
the same responsibility for preparing

430
00:19:59,100 --> 00:20:00,620
and packing for the trip

431
00:20:00,620 --> 00:20:03,180
and for their behavior on the trip

432
00:20:03,180 --> 00:20:04,820
as any other scout would,

433
00:20:04,820 --> 00:20:06,460
Even though you're gonna be there.

434
00:20:06,460 --> 00:20:09,100
you're volunteering to help all the scouts there

435
00:20:09,100 --> 00:20:11,420
and it's really not fair for them

436
00:20:11,420 --> 00:20:15,940
to depend upon you as a parent in that situation.

437
00:20:15,940 --> 00:20:17,420
Now, all. that being said,

438
00:20:17,420 --> 00:20:20,100
you also may wanna arrange for some specific time

439
00:20:20,100 --> 00:20:21,260
within a scouting activity

440
00:20:21,260 --> 00:20:23,780
where you switch from scouter to parent

441
00:20:23,780 --> 00:20:27,220
and you get to be your child's parent for a little while.

442
00:20:27,220 --> 00:20:29,300
Maybe this is a few minutes before bedtime

443
00:20:29,300 --> 00:20:30,940
or just after a meal,

444
00:20:30,940 --> 00:20:33,700
but you can both walk away from the group for a moment,

445
00:20:33,700 --> 00:20:35,260
address any concerns,

446
00:20:35,260 --> 00:20:38,300
share your thoughts with one another as parent and child,

447
00:20:38,300 --> 00:20:41,060
and then go back to your scout and scouter roles.

448
00:20:41,060 --> 00:20:44,140
What we do as parents affects only our children.

449
00:20:44,140 --> 00:20:47,300
What we do as scouters affects other people's children.

450
00:20:47,300 --> 00:20:48,660
If we understand the difference

451
00:20:48,660 --> 00:20:50,980
and prepare our children and ourselves

452
00:20:50,980 --> 00:20:54,260
to observe the boundaries between those two roles

453
00:20:54,260 --> 00:20:56,340
of being a parent and a scouter,

454
00:20:56,340 --> 00:20:59,379
it's very likely we're gonna enjoy scouting together

455
00:20:59,379 --> 00:21:02,919
and better help, all of our scouts at the same time.

456
00:21:04,020 --> 00:21:07,340
["Skouts at the Same Time"].

457
